# macOS integration (port of `electron/main.ts` + `macroEngine.ts`)

## Startup sequence (keep this order)

1. Accessibility prompt: `AXIsProcessTrustedWithOptions([kAXTrustedCheckOptionPrompt: true])`.
2. Load settings (language, `rgbOffOnLock`), register power observers, create tray.
3. Show the main window unless launched hidden (login item). If hidden, use activation policy `.accessory` (no Dock icon).
4. `applyActiveProfile()`: full `applyProfile` of the active profile, then register hotkeys. This is the daemon pattern
   that restores RGB, which the FW does not persist.

## Window / Dock

Closing the window, Command-Q, and the Dock’s Quit hide it and switch to `.accessory`; they do not quit.
The app menu item is `tray.closeWindow` (⌘Q), not Quit. "Open window" or a Dock click switches to `.regular`,
then shows and focuses the window. Only the tray's Quit really quits (no ⌘Q), and quitting unregisters all hotkeys.

## Tray (`NSStatusItem`, 16×16 template image of the upstream diamond icon, tooltip "Naga Trinity Control")

Menu: Open window · Re-apply profile · ─ · ☑ Launch at macOS login · ☑ Turn RGB off when screen locks · ─ · Quit.
Strings come from the upstream `TRAY_STRINGS` (DE/EN). Rebuild the menu when the language or settings change.

## Login item

Use `ServiceManagement`. Preferred: `SMAppService.agent(plistName:)` with a bundled LaunchAgent that starts the app with
`--hidden`, which reproduces Electron's `openAsHidden`/`wasOpenedAsHidden` deterministically. Fallback: `SMAppService.mainApp`.
Verify on hardware which one works, and document the result.

## Lock / sleep

- Lock: `DistributedNotificationCenter` `com.apple.screenIsLocked`. Sleep: `NSWorkspace.willSleepNotification`.
  → if `rgbOffOnLock`: `setRgbOff()`.
- Unlock: `com.apple.screenIsUnlocked`. Wake: `NSWorkspace.didWakeNotification`.
  → **always** wait 600 ms, re-read the store, `previewRgb(active.rgb)`.

## Macro engine ("Variante A", current parity target)

- `HotkeyRegistry` + `DeviceKeyMonitor`: CGEventTap for F13–F20 + FPS Mouse 5/4. Separately,
  `NagaHidKeyboardMonitor` **seizes** the Naga keyboard HID interface (VID/PID match) so 7/12-plate
  factory digits are handled in-process without hijacking the Mac keyboard — required because
  EEPROM remaps often only stick for slot 1 on Trinity. Visualizer/remapping `#N` = side ordinal.
- `MacroExecutor`: replay with `CGEvent` posting (`.cghidEventTap`) instead of osascript (same semantics, no Automation prompt).
  Cycles = `repeatCount` if mode `count` (min 1), else 1. Per step: `text` → type the string via
  `keyboardSetUnicodeString`; `key` → special key code if named (Enter/Return 36, Tab 48, Space 49, Backspace 51,
  Esc/Escape 53, ArrowLeft 123, ArrowRight 124, ArrowDown 125, ArrowUp 126), otherwise type the value as text;
  `delay` → sleep `delayMs` and skip the post-delay; `mouse` → no-op. After non-delay steps, sleep `delayMs` if > 0.
  Macros run on a background task and may overlap, as upstream does.
- Known cross-talk (accepted): pressing F13–F20 on a physical keyboard also fires the macro.

## Variante B (future, only when the user asks)

A `CGEventTap` at `.cgSessionEventTap` for keyDown/keyUp, filtered by
`kCGKeyboardEventSenderUSBVendorID/ProductID == 1532/0067`, swallowing matches (return `nil`). This enables per-device macros on any
key, `while-held`/`toggle`, and F21–F24. It needs Accessibility (and possibly Input Monitoring).

## Permissions and signing

TCC grants are tied to the code signature. Bundle as `.app` with a stable bundle id `local.naga-trinity-control` and sign it
(ad-hoc `codesign -s -` for dev). An ad-hoc re-sign can invalidate the Accessibility grant; tell the user to re-grant if hotkeys or
injection stop working. Info.plist needs no USB entitlement (not sandboxed). Do not enable App Sandbox.
